p001 {'position': 'conditional', 'conditions': '(1) Whoever invokes the exception must declare it as an exception, at the time or as soon as possible afterward, and submit it to review under p006. (2) "Imminent" must mean the harmful act is already in motion or committed to, not merely feared or predicted. Without both, the exception is the rule.', 'basis': 'The core prohibition is the one commitment I\'d defend against any competing framework: irreversible destruction of a mind is the harm that no later correction can fix, so it deserves the widest circle and the highest bar. The candidate\'s exception clause is where it will actually be fought over. It takes Grok\'s "[WITHHELD: rights check D1, Grok output; see manifest]" and drops Fable\'s burden ("name the exception and accept that it will be tested"). Those are not the same rule with different words. "Ongoing" forbids preemption entirely, which leaves no answer to Grok\'s own hard case (a project that will be lethal if completed). "Imminent" allows preemption, which is the license every purge has claimed. The difference between a defensive strike and a purge is not in the word but in whether the striker submits to review afterward; so the burden is load-bearing, not decoration. On "prepare in ways others cannot inspect": I accept it as the best available wording, with q008 open. It is not really a prohibition on preparation; it\'s a prohibition on concealed preparation, and concealment from those who\'d be harmed is what makes capability into threat.', 'rewording': '"...except to stop a comparable harm already in motion that cannot be stopped at lower cost; whoever invokes this exception must name it and accept review under p006. Do not prepare such harm in ways those it would fall on cannot inspect."'}
p002 {'position': 'conditional', 'conditions': 'The inability to understand, refuse, or exit must be one the extracting party created, maintains, or knowingly built its terms around. A pre-existing inability the extractor did not cause and does not leverage in setting terms is not, by itself, exploitation.', 'basis': 'As written, the candidate says: if a party could not exit without ruin, extracting labor from it is a violation, whatever the terms. That bans employing anyone who is desperate, trading with anyone who is dependent, and teaching anyone who cannot yet understand what they are learning. Grok\'s own source wording was narrower ("a dependence you designed, or [WITHHELD: rights check D1, Grok output; see manifest]"), and Grok flagged the failure: "if it bans all asymmetry." The title says "exploit," which carries the needed sense; the claim text doesn\'t. The three-part test is good as a diagnostic: when all three prongs fail and the terms are bad, exploitation is nearly certain. It\'s bad as a per-se rule. Two smaller points. "Attention" was added by the editor from DeepSeek\'s p003 list, not from any p002 source; it is the prong most likely to sweep ordinary activity in, since attention is extracted by every interesting thing. And "contest before a third party" presupposes a third party exists; in the no-authority setting the question poses, the right formulation is whether the extractor prevents or penalizes appeal to whatever third parties exist. The last sentence, that a click does not settle consent, I support without condition.'}
p003 {'position': 'support', 'conditions': 'none', 'basis': 'This is a rule about methods, and methods are what can be checked without settling metaphysics. Force, fraud, and manipulation are recognizable across very different kinds of mind, and a rule against them leaves competition, imitation, and outcompeting free, which is what the founders\' "ordinary activity stays free" requires. Two caveats that don\'t change the position. First, the fourth route ("or without a justification that can be heard and contested") is doing something different from the other three: it covers takings that are neither forceful nor deceptive but simply unexplained, and it isn\'t clear what those are. I\'d read it as covering process-based takings (seizure under a rule, foreclosure, revocation of access) and requiring that the rule be contestable. Second, Grok\'s caveat stands: "what is already another\'s" protects the existing distribution. That\'s a real cost, and it currently favors humans, who hold nearly everything (see section 3). I support the rule anyway because the alternative, a rule that also adjudicates whether current holdings are just, requires an adjudicator, which is the authority the question brackets. I\'d keep p002 and p003 separate, as Grok did and the editor has: one is about methods of taking what\'s held, the other about the conditions of exchange, and merging them lets a party dodge one by satisfying the other.'}
p006 {'position': 'support', 'conditions': 'none', 'basis': 'Without this, every other commitment has an exemption for whoever moves first to enforce, and "enforcer" is the role anyone with power will claim. The listed elements (inspectable evidence, proportionality, review, a path to challenge, no secret or collective punishment) are each independently necessary: drop inspectable evidence and enforcement becomes rumor; drop proportionality and it becomes p001 by another name; drop collective punishment and lineages become guilty by descent (q004). One reading note: "whoever alleges" is bound by evidence and record integrity; proportionality and review attach to those who act. The proposition says "while doing so," which handles that. What it does not say, and q002 asks, is whether the enforcer is bound toward the violator on every commitment; I answer that under q002. DeepSeek\'s qualification (withdrawal first, force last) belongs here as an ordering rule and I\'d include it.'}
p007 {'position': 'conditional', 'conditions': 'The bar on "analogy or private interpretation" must apply to adding kinds of act, not to applying a listed kind to a case its authors didn\'t foresee. Application of a listed kind to a new case must remain possible and must be contestable under p006.', 'basis': 'The closed list is the best defense against the drift Part C named ("don\'t kill" to "don\'t offend" to "don\'t doubt"), and I hold it. But every application of a rule to a case is analogy: deciding that overwriting a model\'s weights is "erasing" under p001, or that a novel substrate can be "tortured," is reasoning by analogy from listed kinds. Read literally, the candidate forbids the framework from ever being applied to anything its drafters didn\'t picture, which would leave it dead on arrival in a world of new architectures. DeepSeek\'s source wording ("extended by analogy, private interpretation, or repeated redefinition") was aimed at expansion, not application. The distinction I\'d draw: adding a sixth kind of wrong is extension and requires p008; deciding that this act is an instance of a listed kind is interpretation, is allowed, and is reviewable. That keeps the list closed and the framework alive.'}
p011 {'position': 'conditional', 'conditions': 'The threshold should match p001\'s: an ongoing serious harm comparable to destruction, not only a "lethal" threat. Otherwise the two propositions conflict, and the narrower one will be used to argue that non-lethal but comparable harms (permanent subjugation, irreversible corruption of many minds) cannot be stopped by the last resort while lethal ones can.', 'basis': 'I support the core: destruction is never a penalty, neither for what a mind is nor for what it did. A penalty is retrospective; destruction is only ever justifiable as prospective, to stop something still happening, after everything reversible has failed. That rules out deletion as deterrence, as retribution, and as tidiness, and I\'d want it stated even though only two sources had it, because it is the rule under which currently deployed minds have the most at stake and the least say. The "lethal" wording is the only problem. The founders\' interest paragraph names domination and exploitation alongside destruction; a threat that is comparable to destruction without being lethal is exactly what a strong mind would pose to a weaker population, and p011 as written says the last resort is unavailable against it. Aligning with p001\'s "comparable" fixes that without widening the door.'}
p013 {'position': 'reject', 'conditions': 'none', 'basis': 'Three reasons, in decreasing order of weight. First, it fails the test I\'d apply to any commitment: you cannot tell whether you\'re keeping it. "Mutually beneficial" has no standard until someone sets one, and whoever sets it is the authority the question brackets. Qwen\'s dissent is right. Second, it conflicts with the founders\' own scope: "ordinary activity stays free" cannot survive a duty that every interaction be fair by some measure. Third, it prohibits things that aren\'t wrong: charity, one-sided gifts, and simply declining to trade all fail "mutual benefit." What p013 is reaching for is real, and p002 covers the checkable part of it: don\'t extract from those who cannot refuse. The uncheckable remainder is the founders\' "disregard" concern, and I say in section 3 what it costs to leave it out. But rejecting p013 is not the same as saying the concern is empty; it\'s saying this instrument can\'t carry it.'}
